{"decision": "repair", "edits": [{"old": "initial	q_cts_n  = 1'b1;
	initial	qq_cts_n = 1'b1;
	initial	ck_cts   = 1'b0;
	always	@(posedge i_clk)
		{ qq_cts_n, q_cts_n } <= { q_cts_n, i_cts_n };
	always	@(posedge i_clk)
		ck_cts <= (!qq_cts_n)||(!hw_flow_control);", "new": "initial	q_cts_n  = 1'b1;
	initial	qq_cts_n = 1'b1;
	initial	ck_cts   = 1'b1;
	always	@(posedge i_clk)
		{ qq_cts_n, q_cts_n } <= { q_cts_n, i_cts_n };
	always	@(posedge i_clk)
		ck_cts <= (!qq_cts_n)||(!hw_flow_control);"}], "explanation": "The RTL initializes the CTS synchronizer chain to 1'b1,1'b1,0. After reset (and BREAK) the synchronizer requires two clocks to output 0 when the receiver holds CTS high (active-low). The contract requires that after eight synchronizer clocks the transmitter must still see the receiver as blocked. With the original initialization, ck_cts becomes 0 at cycle 2, causing o_busy to drop and a byte to be accepted while the receiver still blocks. Initializing ck_cts to 1 delays the first 'CTS permissive' observation by one cycle, extending the enforced busy period through the required eight synchronizer warm-up clocks after BREAK recovery, which eliminates the CTS violations without changing any other logic."}
